7 true] [17592186280641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534177 true] [17592186280641 1111 #uuid "a932a83a-9095-5f0e-8e23-49741ae40df6" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280642 1034 "The sequence diverges" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280642 1111 #uuid "e79fae98-a615-5226-8fce-b5e94bc346e2" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280643 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280644 13194139534177 true] [17592186280643 1089 "q-123714" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280643 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534177 true] [17592186280643 1100 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280643 1113 #uuid "07f019fc-52c4-5337-bbad-e5807016d73f" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280643 1145 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280645 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280646 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280647 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280648 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280649 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1041 "selection" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280647 13194139534177 true] [17592186280644 1110 #uuid "4c124ee9-94cf-5539-b964-4c65589210ec" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280645 1034 "\\frac{1}{10}" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280645 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534177 true] [17592186280645 1111 #uuid "04fc6693-9aa3-5041-96df-f89d92ba5ae4" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280646 1034 "10" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534177 true] [17592186280646 1111 #uuid "410c7c1d-3d7a-5045-a258-e543f110a569" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280647 1034 "The sequence diverges." 13194139534177 true] [17592186280647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280647 1111 #uuid "6f047844-de48-5145-a031-3fc7f046f591" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280648 1034 "0" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534177 true] [17592186280648 1111 #uuid "f6b04115-b3fc-5205-8e9a-02656bf30804" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280649 1034 "-1" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534177 true] [17592186280649 1111 #uuid "3f1c2eaa-6b51-5f14-8ed0-764d61f58df9" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280650 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280651 13194139534177 true] [17592186280650 1089 "q-49734" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280650 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534177 true] [17592186280650 1100 "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n>1,$ diverge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}}$" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280650 1113 #uuid "57946c6b-cbfd-57f6-9f11-08ecdcc2f113" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280650 1145 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\"" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280652 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280653 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280654 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280655 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280656 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1041 "selection" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280656 13194139534177 true] [17592186280651 1110 #uuid "4046cbdf-257a-5bdd-adbd-f63ad89feeef" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280652 1034 "${b}_{n}$ only" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280652 1111 #uuid "c45892a5-fa4e-59b5-977d-61eb272cef33" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280653 1034 "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280653 1111 #uuid "234e8662-817b-5e07-a98b-ffb415f965a4" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280654 1034 "${c}_{n}$ only" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280654 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280654 1111 #uuid "25faa093-7811-5eec-bd3d-f873e1105f6f" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280655 1034 "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280655 1111 #uuid "6ad4103d-49aa-55ff-a691-1c7ced9bf9fd" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280656 1034 "${a}_{n}$ only" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534177 true] [17592186280656 1111 #uuid "abf315db-c1da-52cd-a7bd-0fe0bfe090de" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280657 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280658 13194139534177 true] [17592186280657 1089 "q-75948" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280657 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534177 true] [17592186280657 1100 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280657 1113 #uuid "a321a1e4-d59b-5239-a7ef-bd1350c42b74" 13194139534177 true] [17592186280657 1145 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=10+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." 13194139534177 true] [17592186280658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280659 13194139534177 true] [17592186280658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280660 13194139534177 true] [17592186280658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280661 13194139534177 true] [17592186280658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280662 131941395